WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.740
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:11.580
Wow. This is a historic moment today. Let&nbsp;
me tell you why. DeepMind just introduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:17.580
their new system called AlphaDev and it&nbsp;
is based on something they call AlphaZero.&nbsp;

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:22.920
AlphaZero was an AI system that&nbsp;
could learn to play Chess, Go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:30.960
and Shogi by playing against itself. So&nbsp;
how good is it? Well, it is incredible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:37.380
And I really mean that, this is an AI that&nbsp;
can beat world champions in these games.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:42.360
Now I hope you allow me to quote&nbsp;
myself from about 400 videos ago:&nbsp;

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.660
“The point of AlphaZero is not to solve Chess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:52.800
or any of these games. Its main point is to&nbsp;
show that a general AI can be created that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:59.520
can perform on a superhuman level on not one,&nbsp;
but several different tasks at the same time.“

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:07.620
It can learn as long as we formulate the problem&nbsp;
as a video game. For instance, it is easy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:13.380
think about Chess like we think about a game,&nbsp;
because that’s exactly what it is. If you win,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:20.040
you get some points, if you lose, you lose some&nbsp;
points. Simple. However, what their new AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:27.600
AlphaDev does is computer coding. Excuse&nbsp;
me, what? How is coding a video game?

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:34.980
Yes, it really imagines computer coding as a video&nbsp;
game. You see, in the case of sorting numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:41.280
the computer game level will be a series of&nbsp;
numbers, and the actions that we perform with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:48.120
a controller in a game will be writing code. If&nbsp;
this code is written well, it will sort these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:56.100
numbers correctly, in which case, oh yes, that’s&nbsp;
right, we get a nice score. But let’s add a twist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:03.300
to the game: latency. Correctness is necessary,&nbsp;
but the faster the code, the higher the score.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:10.920
I love it. Coding as a video game. That&nbsp;
sounds nice. However, I doubt it will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:17.940
able to say anything new as sorting is a very&nbsp;
well studied area in computer science. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:23.880
one of the first things every computer scientist&nbsp;
starts learning about and there are hundreds and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:29.220
hundreds of algorithms published in this area.&nbsp;
I don’t expect that this will be able to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:36.000
anything truly new here, but if it can at least&nbsp;
match what humans can do, that would be nice.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:44.460
And…what? Look at that! Am I dreaming&nbsp;
or is this real? On shorter sequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:54.420
it is up to 70% faster, and even on big workloads,&nbsp;
it is about 1.7% faster than the currently used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.420 --> 00:03:00.660
techniques. My goodness! And if that is at&nbsp;
all possible, it gets even better. Get this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:08.160
they have deployed it into the LLVM libc++&nbsp;
sorting library. What does that mean? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:14.040
hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars, because&nbsp;
it likely means that that it is now being used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:21.360
more than a trillions times a day. Wow. If&nbsp;
someone finds out something new and useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:27.780
in the area of physical manufacturing, it might&nbsp;
very well take decades for a paper to see any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:34.560
use in the real world. However, here, we are&nbsp;
going from paper to product not in decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:40.320
and not even in years. This is&nbsp;
tech transfer in a matter of days.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:46.080
And this is an AI that can say, I got&nbsp;
this, don’t worry about it. Just tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.240
me what you wish to see, and I’ll write&nbsp;
this software on my own. And don’t forget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:58.740
this is a difficult game. It is like constructing&nbsp;
a skyscraper…from scratch! One wrong move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:04.560
and you are done. And it is not just&nbsp;
as good as the collective intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:10.740
of humanity, but it even exceeds it.&nbsp;
My goodness. What a time to be alive!

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:18.540
And interestingly, it also doesn’t write&nbsp;
C++, but it writes Assembly code directly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:23.760
this roughly means that it is really low to&nbsp;
the ground. This is a bit closer to the metal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:30.480
the hardware itself. Proper coding skills&nbsp;
from an AI that even rivals humans.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:33.420
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

